# What the Racists Desire

People oppressed in European countries crossed the sea, determined to gain a life of rights. The broad American continent gave them a rare home. Rejoicing in it as God's grace, they prospered and proudly claimed religious freedom and honourable rights. Learned people delighted as labour transformed forest into country.

Great plantations arose; cotton and sugarcane flourished. Wealth increased, hearts hardened, riches bred arrogance, and virtue disappeared. Plantation owners wanted labourers cheaply. Treating people as animals, they hunted Africans, seized human beings given life by God, called them slaves, and sold them in markets. Crime became an industry.

As the Black population grew, white people became more fanatical. They declared Black men their slaves and Black women their property. Young Black women became sugarcane for sexual predators. Chastity, they claimed, belonged only to whites; God had graciously supplied Black slaves to hold their limbs, labour hard, accept whatever was given, and submit. Though human in form, Africans were called mindless animals.

Black people laboured until their spirits failed, turning the new country into a land that yielded gold. Enslaved young women were displayed and sold in markets for their bodies. Born and raised elsewhere, captured by strangers, they laboured until body and heart were ruined. Priests rolled rosaries in new churches while a father cried, “My daughter!” Masters replied that father and daughter were merely human customs: to an animal she was only its offspring, and a slave had no right to speak of rights.

Masters ordered enslaved people to strip because clothes concealed the body and reduced its value. Resistance meant death. Those fleeing cruelty into forests were hunted with dogs, whipped to death, or hanged. Law could not intervene because a slave was property and the owner's desire ruled. God did not listen; he remained in a church built by a slave-master.

Lincoln, a courageous man of action with justice in his heart, defeated the idlers and freed those in this miserable condition. Slavery and market sale ended; law declared Black people human beings, not property. Yet the demonic heart remained. Colour difference became racist frenzy.

Kennedy too, who boldly challenged this madness, fell to murder. The government said equal rights must be given to Black people so his soul might rest, and passed a law. Fanatics responded with cruel violence to overthrow it. Before their mind's eye stood the enslaved young woman; they longed for that old order to return.

Since appeals to virtue and justice did not restrain them, Black people rose with strength, answering blow with blow and kick with kick. Turmoil spread in America, but they proclaimed that through it cruelty would be destroyed, and the world praised their struggle.

---NOTES---
The poem moves from European settlement through slavery, emancipation, segregation, Kennedy's murder, and the civil-rights struggle. Historical racial terminology in the source is translated contextually and not reproduced as contemporary endorsement.

